<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1888 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Sansoen Phra Barami</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="th">สรรเสริญพระบารมี</bdi> / <bdi lang="th-Latn">Sansoen Phra Barami</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The royal anthem of Thailand, played for the king and before films and shows. It was Siam's national anthem until 1932.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Prince Narisara Nuwattiwong; revised by King Vajiravudh</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Pyotr Schurovsky, on the tune chosen in 1871</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>The kingdom of Siam, later Thailand</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Thai</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Siam (present-day Thailand)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>tune chosen 1871; sheet music printed 1888; words 1886, revised 1914</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Prince Narisara Nuwattiwong (1886), revised by King Vajiravudh (1914); melody printed in 1888)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Sansoen Phra Barami&quot; (&quot;Praise the King&quot;) is the royal anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, and before 1932 it served as the de facto national anthem of Siam. Its history begins in the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Mongkut</a>. In 1851 two former British officers, Captain Impey and Lieutenant Thomas George Knox, trained the troops of King Mongkut and the Second King Pinklao in the British military tradition and adopted &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot; as the honour music for the king of Siam. Phraya Srisunthonwohan (Noi Āchāryānkura) later wrote Thai words for it under the name &quot;Chom Rat Chong Charoen&quot;.</p><p>In 1871 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a> visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> (now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>) and found that Siam was using the same anthem as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, which then ruled Singapore. Siam needed a tune of its own. A group of Siamese traditional musicians chose &quot;Bulan Loi Luean&quot;, a composition of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-ii">Rama II</a>, and Chulalongkorn ordered Christopher Hewetson, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> bandmaster in the Royal Siamese Army, to arrange it in western style for military band. The record of the anthem after 1871 is thin. In 1888 sheet music of the Siamese national anthem, arranged by the Russian composer Pyotr Schurovsky, was printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; its main melody is the tune played today, and research by Sugree Charoensuk of Mahidol University holds that Schurovsky composed the music to serve as Siam's national anthem.</p><p>Prince Narisara Nuwattiwong wrote several sets of words for the tune: the present words in 1886, a version for the army in 1888, three versions adopted in the schools of Siam in 1902, and words in the play Sang Thong; Prince Abhakara Kiartivongse wrote a version for the navy. In 1914 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajiravudh">Vajiravudh</a> set the other versions aside and revised Narisara's words into the present text. The Wild Tiger Corps and Siamese scouts first sang it on 22 January 1914 at Ban Bo Suphan, on a march from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-pathom">Nakhon Pathom</a> to the pagoda at Don Chedi, and the Ministry of Education adopted it for schools nationwide that year. The tune had also been played in two forms, the full version for the king and a half version for the Prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/front-palace">Front Palace</a>, a custom that ended with the death of the last holder of that office, Prince Wichaichan, in 1885. In 1900 a Siamese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> group visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> performed the anthem for the ethnomusicologist Carl Stumpf, who made its first recording on an Edison wax cylinder.</p><p>After the Siamese Revolution of 1932 a new national anthem, &quot;Phleng Chat Siam&quot;, replaced it, and &quot;Sansoen Phra Barami&quot; remained an honorific song for the king. Its music was shortened for ceremonial use in the western manner under regulations dated 4 February 1935. In 1940 the government of Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plaek-phibunsongkhram">Plaek Phibunsongkhram</a>, by the 8th Thai cultural mandate of 26 April, shortened the words and replaced &quot;Siam&quot; with &quot;Thai&quot;. These short versions were quietly abandoned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and the 1914 text returned.</p><p>The anthem is performed on state occasions and when a senior member of the royal family is present. It is played before films in cinemas and before plays, concerts and most other live performances in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, and at the start and close of television and radio broadcasting. A royal regulation published in the Royal Thai Government Gazette in 2019 sets out when it is played: for the king, the queen, the Princess Royal, heirs to the throne and royal remains, and when certain royal standards are raised.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of honour to the king on behalf of his subjects, with wishes for his long and prosperous reign. The mood is solemn and devotional.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Thai</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Thai</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">ข้าวรพุทธเจ้า</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">Kha wora phutthachao</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We, servants of Your Majesty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">เอามโนและศิระกราน</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">ao mano lae sira kran</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>bow down with heart and head</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">นบพระภูมิบาล บุญดิเรก</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">nop phra phumiban bun direk</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to pay homage to the protector of the land, of surpassing merit,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">เอกบรมจักริน</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">ek borom chakkrin</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the one supreme Chakri,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">พระสยามินทร์</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">phra sayamin</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>great lord of Siam,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">พระยศยิ่งยง</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">phra yot ying yong</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whose glory is exceeding and lasting.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">เย็นศิระเพราะพระบริบาล</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">yen sira phro phra boriban</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Our heads are cool because of your royal care;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">ผลพระคุณ ธ รักษา</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">phon phra khun tha raksa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>by the fruit of the grace with which you guard us</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">ปวงประชาเป็นสุขศานต์</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">puang pracha pen suk san</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>all the people are happy and at peace.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">ขอบันดาล</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">kho bandan</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We pray that</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">ธ ประสงค์ใด จงสฤษดิ์ดัง</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">tha prasong dai chong sarit dang</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whatever you desire be fulfilled as</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">หวังวรหฤทัย</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">wang wora haruethai</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your noble heart hopes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="th">ดุจถวายชัย ชโย</span><span class="sg-r" lang="th-Latn">dut thawai chai chayo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as we offer you victory: chayo!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Prince Narisara Nuwattiwong, revised by King Vajiravudh (1914); the Thai text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sansoen_Phra_Barami" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (Royal Thai General System of Transcription (RTGS)).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>line 1: 'kha', servant, with 'wora phutthachao', literally 'excellent Buddha-lord', the royal form of address: the subjects speak of themselves as the king's servants.</li><li>line 4: 'chakkrin', the Chakri king (the dynasty's name, also 'wheel-bearer', a universal monarch).</li><li>line 5: 'Sayamin', lord (Indra) of Siam.</li><li>line 7: 'cool head' is a Thai image of peace and safety.</li><li>line 8: 'tha' (ธ) is the royal pronoun 'he' or 'you'.</li><li>line 13: 'chayo', 'victory!', the cheer that ends the anthem.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Abridged words under the 8th cultural mandate (Fine Arts Department)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1940</span><span class="sg-chain-h">shortened words, &quot;Siam&quot; replaced by &quot;Thai&quot;</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1851</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Siam's royal honour music before this anthem</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Chom Rat Chong Charoen (Phraya Srisunthonwohan)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Thai words to God Save the King, the earlier royal anthem</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Bulan Loi Luean (King Rama II)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">tune chosen as the new anthem in 1871</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sansoen Sua Pa (anthem of the Wild Tiger Corps)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1911</span><span class="sg-chain-h">may share the same tune, by Sugree Charoensuk's research</span></li></ul></section>
